  have — a task model, multiple users with identity, a notion of "caring about" a task
  (subscription/assignment/watching), and change detection. A notifications system can't be specified on top of nothing,
  so either we're designing those too, or you have an existing app in mind that I'm not seeing.

  [1mThe fork that decides everything downstream:[0m whether notifications are a [3mclient-side[0m concern or a [3mserver-side[0m one.

  - [1mBrowser-only, single user[0m — notifications are derived from local state changes in one tab. Cheap, no backend, but
    "users" is really one user, and nothing reaches them when the tab is closed. Rules out email/push later without a
    rewrite.
  - [1mMulti-user with a backend[0m — real user identity, a server that owns task state and emits change events, delivery
    channels (in-app, email, push). This is the only shape where "users get notified" means what it usually means. Much
    larger; the notifications system becomes one subsystem of a real app.
  - [1mNotifications layer for an app that exists elsewhere[0m — this repo is a stub and the real app is somewhere else. Then
    I need to see that codebase; the design keys entirely off its stack.

  Cheap to change later: which channels we deliver on, notification copy, grouping/digest rules. Expensive: the
  client/server split and the user-identity model — picking wrong means redoing the whole thing.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m if this repo is the whole app, start with browser-only in-app notifications over a local task
  model, and design the change-event boundary cleanly so a server can be slotted behind it later. Building auth + a
  backend + push delivery before there's a single task on the page is a lot of machinery for nothing to notify about.
